import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Text operations - Analysis-Library.md §2.2 and Part 6 item 5, Implementation-String.md part 5.
   ==========================================================================================

   `String.yana` is what a string *is*; this is what a program does with one. Search, splitting,
   trimming, the ASCII predicates and the ASCII case tier, plus the two whole-string case
   conversions - the family Analysis-Library.md §2.2 collapses `Text.h`'s cross product of
   {`char`, `WChar`, `WChar32`} x {null-terminated, view, buffer} x {const, mutable} down to.
   There is one code unit, one view type, and `&` is a convention rather than a type, so every axis
   of that product is already gone and what is left is one function per operation.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Three files, and what decides which one a function is written in

   `Text.native.yana` and `Text.js.yana` hold the operations whose two answers *have nothing in
   common* - Analysis-Modules.md §2.5. That set is exactly the scans: natively a scan is the vector
   search the bulk operations already have (`indexOf` and `occurrences` over `Chunked(String,
   CodeUnit)`, sixteen or thirty-two units at a time behind one `firstSet`), and on JS it is
   `String.prototype.indexOf`, which Implementation-String.md part 5 rules is the right
   implementation rather than a compromise: a fixed run of units has no room for the cross-engine
   disagreement the decoding tier has to worry about, and the engine's own C++ beats any
   `charCodeAt` loop this compiler emits.

   Everything else is here, once, because it is *composed* of those - `trim` is two bounded walks
   and a slice, `splitOn` is a search in a loop, `String.replace` is a search and two appends. A
   second copy of one of those per target would be a second chance to disagree about an edge case
   for no speed at all.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why this tier is ASCII and what that buys

   Nothing below decodes anything, and nothing below has to: **an ASCII unit is self-synchronizing in
   both UTF-8 and UTF-16** - a value below 0x80 never appears inside the encoding of any other code
   point in either. So a scan for one ASCII unit, a split on one, a trim against a set of them and a
   case conversion restricted to `A`-`Z` all mean the same thing in both builds and land on the same
   indices for ASCII content. That is a real property of both encodings rather than something being
   leaned on loosely, and it is what lets one signature over `CodeUnit` serve a byte scan natively
   and a UTF-16 scan on JS.

   The operations that take a *whole string* as the thing looked for - `String.find`, `startsWith`,
   `endsWith`, `String.contains`, `String.replace` - carry no such restriction and need none: they
   compare runs of raw units, and encoding is an injective function of content, so they answer the
   same on both targets for any content at all.

   **The case tier is `A`-`Z` and `a`-`z` and nothing else**, deliberately, and the name of every
   function that does it says `Ascii`. Unicode case folding changes unit *counts* (German `ß` becomes
   `SS`) and is locale-sensitive, so a host `toLowerCase()` is not the same function on the two
   targets and is not this one on either. The Unicode tier is Analysis-Library.md §2.3 and is layered
   on this without changing it.

   Positions are `Size` and count this target's own units, exactly as `String.unit` and `length` do -
   the same number on both targets for ASCII content and deliberately not for anything else
   (Implementation-String.md part 3).
-}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   One unit, classified - Analysis-Library.md §2.2's predicate row.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `Int` rather than `CodeUnit`, because `String.unit` answers an `Int` and these exist to be written
   next to it: `isSpace(text.unit(at))` is the expression they are for. A unit that is not ASCII
   answers `False` to every one of them bar `isControl`, which is what makes them total rather than
   partial - there is no unit these are undefined for, so no caller needs a range test in front of
   one.

   Written as comparisons rather than as a table lookup, which is what the C++ they replace does too:
   two compares and an `and` beat a load, and the optimizer folds the pair into one unsigned compare
   where the range is contiguous - `unit - 48 <= 9` is the whole of `isDigit` after
   `foldRangeCompare`.
-}

-- Whether it is an ASCII unit at all, which is the one predicate the rest are only meaningful under.
pub fn isAscii(unit: Int) -> Bool = unit >= 0 && unit < 128

pub fn isDigit(unit: Int) -> Bool = unit >= 48 && unit <= 57
pub fn isUpper(unit: Int) -> Bool = unit >= 65 && unit <= 90
pub fn isLower(unit: Int) -> Bool = unit >= 97 && unit <= 122
pub fn isAlpha(unit: Int) -> Bool = isUpper(unit) || isLower(unit)
pub fn isAlphaNum(unit: Int) -> Bool = isAlpha(unit) || isDigit(unit)

-- Space, tab, newline, vertical tab, form feed and carriage return - the six `isspace` answers `True`
-- for in the C locale, and the set `trim` and `words` are written against. 9 to 13 is contiguous, so
-- this is two comparisons and not six.
pub fn isSpace(unit: Int) -> Bool = unit == 32 || (unit >= 9 && unit <= 13)

pub fn isControl(unit: Int) -> Bool = (unit >= 0 && unit < 32) || unit == 127
pub fn isPrintable(unit: Int) -> Bool = unit >= 32 && unit < 127

{-
   A hexadecimal digit's value, or nothing for a unit that is not one.

   **The four-bit refinement is the whole of why this can be a `Maybe`.** A nibble has sixteen
   values and `@bits(4) U32`'s natural storage has 256, so `ReprTable::intNiche` donates
   `[16, 256)` and the folded `Maybe(HexDigit)` is one byte with `Nothing` written as 16 - no tag
   word, no second register, and a `Just` that is the digit itself. `doc/spec/repr.md` states the
   donation and `Native/Run.yana`'s `Count` is the same refinement used for the same reason.

   So the sentinel did not go away, it stopped being the *caller's* to know: what `-1` used to say
   in the return type this says in the constructor, and the number that stands for absence is the
   layout's business rather than a value in the digit's own range that every reader has to remember
   to test for. `test/resolve/Niche.yana` is where the donation is asserted, in the layout dump and
   behaviourally both.

   `@bits(4)` and not sixteen enum constructors: a refinement never participates in dispatch or in
   pattern matching, so a `Just(v)` binds an ordinary `U32` and arithmetic on it needs no instance of
   its own. That is what keeps `hexValue(u)` composable with the shifts a hexadecimal reader is
   written out of.

   **The layout is free and so, now, is the code.** Over a 64 KiB corpus of five-sixths hexadecimal
   digits at v2, one call per unit, each answer tested and contributed: the sentinel form is 4.58 ns
   per unit and this one is **4.36**, which is 0.95x over three runs. The `Maybe` is not a tax; it is
   marginally the cheaper of the two, because `cmp $0xf ; jbe` against the niche is the same shape as
   `test ; jl` against `-1` while the sentinel additionally holds `0xffffffff` in a register across
   the loop and sign-extends every `Just` on the way out.

   That is the second of two backend fixes and neither was about `Maybe`:

   - `promoteStackSlots` declined any slot whose accesses disagreed about the register type, and a
     folded `Maybe` is read once as the `Int` its discriminant test compares and again as the payload
     type - so every one of them was staying in memory. It holds the wider type and converts at each
     access now;
   - that conversion was a `bitcast`, which says nothing about the bits above the narrow value's
     width, so the mask `narrowedTo` puts on every load could never be folded away: `and $0xff`
     stood between the phi and the discriminant test on every read. A widening integer move is a
     zero extension now - the same instruction on this machine, and one `knownZeroBits` can see
     through. See `carriedInto` in compiler/lower/lower_promote.cpp, which states the argument.

   What is left is the refinement's own narrowing store, an `and $0xf` on each of the two `Just`
   arms. It is `@bits`' contract and the price of the niche, and removing it needs a range analysis
   rather than a known-bits one - the value is in range because the *branch* above it says the unit
   was between `0` and `9`, which is a fact `opt_range.cpp` deliberately does not carry. It is not
   costing anything against the sentinel, so it is a tidiness item rather than a performance one.
-}
pub alias HexDigit = @bits(4) U32

pub fn hexValue(unit: Int) -> Maybe(HexDigit):
    if isDigit(unit) then return Just(truncate(unit - 48) :: HexDigit)

    let lower = toLowerUnit(unit)
    if lower >= 97 && lower <= 102 then return Just(truncate(lower - 87) :: HexDigit)

    return Nothing

pub fn isHexDigit(unit: Int) -> Bool = hexValue(unit) is Just(_)

-- One unit's case, changed only when it is a letter. The bias is 32 in both directions because the
-- two ASCII alphabets are exactly that far apart, which is also why the vector forms below are one
-- masked add rather than a table.
pub fn toLowerUnit(unit: Int) -> Int = if isUpper(unit) then unit + 32 else unit
pub fn toUpperUnit(unit: Int) -> Int = if isLower(unit) then unit - 32 else unit

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Whether a unit is there at all
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The search with its answer thrown away, and the one function in the scanning family that is not
   split: what differs between the targets is what `findAscii` *is*, so this is the same program on
   both and duplicating it would be duplicating nothing.
-}
pub fn containsAscii(self: String, unit: CodeUnit) -> Bool = findAscii(self, unit, 0) is Just(_)

-- And whether a whole needle is, which is `String.find` with its answer thrown away for the same
-- reason. An empty needle is in every string, including the empty one - the convention every string
-- library in use has, and the alternative is a special case at every call site.
pub fn String.contains(haystack: String, needle: String) -> Bool =
    String.find(haystack, needle, 0) is Just(_)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Trimming - Analysis-Library.md §2.2's third operation.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   ASCII whitespace off one end or both. One body for both targets and no vectors anywhere, which is
   a measurement rather than an omission: what a trim walks is the whitespace *run*, which is one or
   two units in every text anyone trims, and a vector loop over a two-unit walk is the setup with
   none of the payoff. The scan stops at the first unit that is not whitespace, so the cost is the
   length of what is removed and not the length of what is kept.

   **The result is a copy**, on the terms `String.slice` states: a native `String` is a run this
   caller does not own and a host one is a primitive, so a view over part of either is not a value
   this language has. A caller that wants the *positions* rather than the text asks with
   `findAsciiNone`, which is what these are written over in spirit and what costs nothing.
-}
pub fn trimStart(text: String) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &at = 0 :: Size
    while at < count && isSpace(text.unit(at)):
        at = at + 1

    return String.slice(text, at, count)

pub fn trimEnd(text: String) -> String:
    let &end = length(text)
    while end > (0 :: Size) && isSpace(text.unit(end - 1)):
        end = end - 1

    return String.slice(text, 0, end)

-- Both ends in one slice rather than two, so that an all-whitespace string is walked once and the
-- text that survives is copied once.
pub fn trim(text: String) -> String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &at = 0 :: Size
    while at < count && isSpace(text.unit(at)):
        at = at + 1

    let &end = count
    while end > at && isSpace(text.unit(end - 1)):
        end = end - 1

    return String.slice(text, at, end)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Splitting - Analysis-Library.md §2.2's "the one that changes shape, and it gets better".
   ==========================================================================================

   The C++ this replaces is `template<class F> void split(StringView, char on, F&& f)`. A callback
   cannot `break`, cannot `return` from the enclosing function and cannot be `?`-ed; an `iter fn`
   *is* a callback with a context pointer, so all three work in the loop body and the traversal is
   written once.

   **Two spellings of every split, and the difference between them is an allocation.**

   - `spansOn` yields the half-open `{from, to}` of each part and allocates nothing at all. This is
     the primitive: the search under it is the vector scan, the loop carries two `Size`s, and a
     caller that only wants to *measure* the parts, or to slice some of them, pays for exactly what
     it takes.
   - `splitOn` yields the parts themselves, which is one `String.slice` - one allocation and one
     block copy - per part.

   Both are here rather than only the second, because the second cannot be built into the first by
   any optimizer: the copy is observable, since a `String` a loop body keeps outlives the text it
   came from. Analysis-Library.md §2.2 asks for the parts to be *views* rooted in the source with
   `Flat(CodeUnit)`, which is the shape the native half could hand over and the JS half cannot - a
   host string has no addressable units - so the portable answer is the span pair, and `String.slice`
   is what turns one into text on either target.

   An empty text yields one empty part, and `n` separators yield `n + 1` parts however they are
   placed. That is what makes `splitOn` and `joinWith` inverses, and it is why nothing here skips an
   empty part; `words` is the function that does, and it says so in its name.
-}
pub iter fn spansOn(text: String, on: CodeUnit) -> {Size, Size}:
    let count = length(text)
    let &start = 0 :: Size
    let &running = True

    while running:
        match findAscii(text, on, start):
            Just(at):
                yield {start, at}
                start = at + 1

            Nothing:
                yield {start, count}
                running = False

pub iter fn splitOn(text: String, on: CodeUnit) -> ->String:
    for {from, to} in spansOn(text, on):
        yield String.slice(text, from, to)

-- The same split on a whole separator rather than one unit. The search is `String.find`, so the
-- separator may be any run of units and an empty one is refused rather than looping forever: a split
-- on nothing is the whole text, which is the answer that composes with `joinWith`.
pub iter fn splitOnText(text: String, on: String) -> ->String:
    let count = length(text)
    let step = length(on)

    if step == (0 :: Size):
        yield copy(text)
    else:
        let &start = 0 :: Size
        let &running = True

        while running:
            match String.find(text, on, start):
                Just(at):
                    yield String.slice(text, start, at)
                    start = at + step

                Nothing:
                    yield String.slice(text, start, count)
                    running = False

{-
   The lines of a text, which is `splitOn` at a newline with the other line ending taken off.

   A trailing carriage return is dropped from each part, so a file written on either convention reads
   the same - the one place in this file where the operation is not simply a split, and the reason it
   is worth a name of its own rather than a `splitOn(text, 10)` at every call site.

   **A trailing newline does not produce a last empty line.** `"a\nb\n"` is two lines and not three,
   and the empty text has no lines at all, which is what every line reader in use answers and what
   makes `lines` idempotent over a file that ends the way files end. That is the one place `lines`
   and `splitOn` deliberately differ.

   The test is `from == count`, and only the *last* span can satisfy it: a span starting at the end
   of the text is the one a trailing separator leaves behind. An empty line in the middle starts
   before the end and is yielded, which is the half a "skip empty parts" rule would get wrong.
-}
pub iter fn lines(text: String) -> ->String:
    let count = length(text)

    for {from, to} in spansOn(text, 10):
        if from < count:
            let &end = to
            if end > from && text.unit(end - 1) == 13 then end = end - 1

            yield String.slice(text, from, end)

{-
   The whitespace-separated words of a text, with runs of whitespace collapsed and no empty parts.

   Not `splitOn(text, 32)`: a split yields a part between every pair of adjacent separators, and what
   a caller asking for words wants from `"a  b"` is two of them rather than three. So this is the
   *skipping* traversal - a walk to the next non-whitespace unit and then a scan to the whitespace
   after it - and it yields nothing at all for a text that is entirely whitespace.
-}
pub iter fn words(text: String) -> ->String:
    let count = length(text)
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    while at < count:
        while at < count && isSpace(text.unit(at)):
            at = at + 1

        if at < count:
            let start = at
            while at < count && !isSpace(text.unit(at)):
                at = at + 1

            yield String.slice(text, start, at)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Case-insensitive ordering - and it is split, so the two halves are in the target files.
   ==========================================================================================

   `compareAsciiCase` is `Text.native.yana`'s and `Text.js.yana`'s, beside `equalsAsciiCase`, and
   what is written here is the contract both answer.

   **Lexicographic in the same sense `Ord(String)` is**, over the units with the ASCII case fold
   applied: the answer is decided by the first position at which the two folded units differ, and by
   the lengths where there is no such position - the shorter string ordering first when it is a
   prefix of the longer. That last half is what a unit comparison alone gets wrong in both
   directions.

   The ordering *at* the differing position is over the folded units read as unsigned, which is
   `toLowerUnit` on the two `Int`s and not a lane comparison: a byte at or above 0x80 is negative in
   the signed compare the case fold is written in, and while that is exactly right for deciding
   whether the fold applies to it, it is exactly wrong for deciding which of two units is larger. So
   the vector half answers *where* and the scalar comparison answers *which way*, which costs one
   pair of unit reads on the one iteration that finds a difference.

   The units before that position compare equal folded, and a unit equal to another under the ASCII
   fold is equal to it under either sign, so nothing about the prefix depends on the reading.
-}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Replacing
   ==========================================================================================

   Every occurrence of `needle` replaced by `replacement`, as a new string.

   One body for both targets, and the host's `replaceAll` is deliberately not under the JS half even
   though it exists and is engine C++. Its *replacement* argument is not literal text - `$&`, `` $` ``,
   `$'` and `$$` are substitution patterns in it - so a program replacing text that happens to contain
   one of those would get a different answer on the two targets. The rule this file is written to is
   that a host call is taken where the host computes the same function, and that is exactly where it
   does not.

   The search under the loop still is the host's on JS, which is where the time goes: this is one
   `String.find` per occurrence plus one block copy per gap between them.

   An empty needle matches nowhere rather than everywhere, which is the choice that keeps this
   terminating and is what `String.find` already says about one.
-}
pub fn String.replace(text: String, needle: String, replacement: String) -> String:
    let step = length(needle)
    let count = length(text)
    if step == (0 :: Size) then return copy(text)

    let &out = String.ofCapacity(count)
    let &start = 0 :: Size
    let &running = True

    while running:
        match String.find(text, needle, start):
            Just(at):
                out.push(String.slice(text, start, at))
                out.push(replacement)
                start = at + step

            Nothing:
                out.push(String.slice(text, start, count))
                running = False

    return out

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Bytes as hexadecimal - and it is split, so the two halves are in the target files.
   ==========================================================================================

   The other direction of `hexValue` above, and the operation every digest, fingerprint and wire
   dump ends in: a run of bytes written out as two units apiece.

   **It was `Digest`'s own function**, which is where it was first wanted rather than where it
   belongs - nothing about writing a byte as two digits is a property of a message digest, and a
   second caller would have had to import one to get it. `Digest.hexOf` is now this one under the
   name it always had.

   `[U8]` and not a container class, and reached by the conversion at the call: a `Sha256Digest` is
   a `[U8 *32]` and becomes a slice where it is written, so this says nothing about widths and
   nothing about containers.

   **Lower case, with no argument to say otherwise.** Every protocol that compares digest text as a
   string - a `Content-MD5` header, a `.sha256` sidecar, git - writes it that way, and a case flag
   would be a branch inside the loop paid for by every caller that does not want one. The upper-case
   form is `toUpperAscii` of this, which is a second pass nobody has asked for yet.

   `pushHex` is the half that is split: natively it is a vector loop into the string's own bytes and
   on JS it is a `pushUnit` pair per byte, and those two have nothing in common (Analysis-Modules.md
   §2.5). `hexOf` is that function with the sink made for it, sized exactly, and is one body.
-}
pub fn hexOf(bytes: [U8]) -> String:
    let &text = String.ofCapacity(length(bytes) * 2)
    pushHex(bytes, text)
    return text

-- One nibble's digit - `0`-`9` then `a`-`f`, as arithmetic rather than as a table: the branch is
-- one `cmp` and a `cmov`, and a table would be a load and a bounds check the compiler cannot
-- remove. It is what the JS half and the native tail are written out of, and the native block form
-- is this same expression applied lanewise.
pub fn hexUnit(nibble: Int) -> Int = if nibble < 10 then 48 + nibble else 87 + nibble
